# Ring the MHI channel doorbell on every queued buffer instead of only when
# the modem asks for it.
#
# Without this the downlink deadlocks under sustained load, in practice
# somewhere past 200 Mbps: the host fills all 127 RX descriptors, the modem
# never re-arms the burst-mode doorbell, and rx stops dead for tens of seconds
# while tx keeps flowing. Captured and analysed in x3000/docs/downlink-stall.md.
#
# The parameter comes from
# target/linux/mediatek/patches-6.12/993-bus-mhi-host-optional-doorbell-write.patch
# and defaults to off, so this line is what enables it on this board.
#
# Why here and not /etc/modules.conf, which also works: that file is a ubox
# conffile, so once a modified copy is on the router sysupgrade preserves it
# and a later image can no longer change the value. Nothing owns or preserves
# a file invented here under /etc/modules.d/, so the image always wins and
# editing this line actually takes effect on the next flash.
#
# Why not the kernel command line: mhi is a loadable module here
# (kmod-mhi-bus) and kmodloader never reads /proc/cmdline, so an
# "mhi.force_db_brst_disable=1" bootarg is silently ignored.
#
# One limitation worth knowing: kmodloader applies /etc/modules.d/ options on
# the boot path only. A manual "modprobe mhi" after an "rmmod mhi" would not
# pick this up - pass the option by hand in that case. The usual A/B test does
# not reload the module, so it is unaffected:
#   echo 0 > /sys/module/mhi/parameters/force_db_brst_disable
#   echo 0000:01:00.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/mhi-pci-generic/unbind
#   echo 0000:01:00.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/mhi-pci-generic/bind
mhi force_db_brst_disable=1
